const fibreData={
  surface:['Soft, diffuse lustre','Short staple fibres can create a subtle halo and a less uniformly reflective surface.','Smooth, even lustre','Continuous filaments often appear smoother and reflect light more uniformly.','Start with observation. Finishes, yarn construction and blends can change how either fabric looks.'],
  crease:['Creases more readily','Cotton commonly retains a hand-formed crease, although weave and finishing affect the result.','Often springs back','Polyester is generally more crease-resistant and may recover more quickly after gentle compression.','This is only a comparative clue. Mercerisation, resin finishes and blended yarns can change recovery.'],
  burn:['Burns without melting','Cotton is combustible and can spread flame, but unlike polyester it does not melt into a plastic bead.','Can melt under heat','Polyester can shrink and melt at temperatures capable of burning skin; molten material may increase contact-burn risk.','Both fibres can burn. Fibre name alone does not determine a garment’s flammability—fabric weight, surface and construction also matter.'],
  lab:['Cellulose under analysis','Microscopy and chemical or spectroscopic tests distinguish cotton from other cellulosic fibres.','Polymer under analysis','Infrared spectroscopy can distinguish polyester and help quantify cotton–polyester blends.','For a valuable saree, blends, or a disputed claim, professional testing is the reliable route.']
};
